---
import Learn from '../../layouts/Learn.astro';
import MemoryFlow from '../../components/learn/MemoryFlow.astro';
import BarChart from '../../components/learn/BarChart.astro';
import { COMPANY, PRICES, TRIAL } from '../../data/site';

// Learn comparison page 4 (s94). Rules: see ai-memory-apps-compared.astro and
// data/pricing.ts. Facts read on 2026-10-13:
// - supermemory.ai: "Works with" ChatGPT, Claude, Cursor, Hermes, MCP, Claude
//   Code; "Build with supermemory"; deploy in your data center, VPC or laptop;
//   "SOC 2 certified, HIPAA compliant and GDPR aligned".
// - supermemory.ai/pricing: Free $0 with $5 credits a month, Pro $19 a month
//   ($20 credits), Max $100, Scale $399; self-hosted on Scale and Enterprise; no
//   statement on storage location or encryption.
// - supermemory.ai/docs/supermemory-mcp/introduction: remote MCP server
//   https://mcp.supermemory.ai/mcp, OAuth sign-in, "no API key is required".
// - GitHub supermemoryai/supermemory: MIT, 31.2k stars; "I use AI tools":
//   plugins for Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, OpenCode; local version via
//   `npx supermemory local` (or a curl install), data in ~/.supermemory.
// No price chart: Supermemory's paid plans are priced for developers (same need
// rule, as on the Pricing page). Chart: the 2026-06-05 Cursor test, as in
// one-memory-across-claude-code-cursor-and-codex.
const CHECKED = '13 October 2026';
const OUTLINE = [
  { id: 'what', title: 'What Supermemory is' },
  { id: 'differ', title: 'Where Zaaheen differs' },
  { id: 'table', title: 'Side by side' },
  { id: 'better', title: 'Where Supermemory is the better choice' },
  { id: 'limits', title: 'Where Zaaheen falls short' },
];
const SM_HOME = 'https://supermemory.ai/';
const SM_PRICING = 'https://supermemory.ai/pricing';
const SM_MCP = 'https://supermemory.ai/docs/supermemory-mcp/introduction';
const SM_GITHUB = 'https://github.com/supermemoryai/supermemory';
---
<Learn
  path="/learn/supermemory-alternative/"
  lede="Supermemory is a memory engine for AI agents, mostly built for developers, with a hosted service and a free local version you start from a terminal. If you want one memory for ChatGPT, Claude and Cursor that installs like a normal app and stays encrypted on your own computer, Zaaheen is built for that instead."
  outline={OUTLINE}
>
  <section id="what">
    <h2>What Supermemory is</h2>
    <p>Supermemory gives AI apps and agents a long-term memory. Its home page lists ChatGPT, Claude,
       Cursor and Claude Code among the tools it works with, and its main offer is for teams building
       with it (<a href={SM_HOME}>Supermemory's site</a>). It says it is SOC 2 certified.</p>
    <p>There are two ways to use it. The hosted service: you add its server, mcp.supermemory.ai, to
       your app and sign in, with no key to copy (<a href={SM_MCP}>its setup page</a>). The free plan
       includes $5 of credits a month and Pro is $19 a month (<a href={SM_PRICING}>its pricing</a>).
       Or the local version: Supermemory is open source, and one terminal command starts it on your
       computer, with your memory in a folder there (<a href={SM_GITHUB}>its GitHub page</a>).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="differ">
    <h2>Where Zaaheen differs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Setup without a terminal.</strong> Zaaheen is a normal installer for Windows and
          Mac. You connect each AI app from its Agents tab, and there are no commands to run.</li>
      <li><strong>Encrypted on your computer.</strong> Zaaheen encrypts your memories on your own
          computer, and our servers never receive them. Supermemory's pages we read don't describe
          encryption for its local version.</li>
      <li><strong>Made for people, not products.</strong> Supermemory's paid plans are written for
          developers building with memory. Zaaheen is one plan for one person: ${PRICES.monthly} a
          month or ${PRICES.yearly} a year after {TRIAL.days} days free.</li>
    </ul>

    <MemoryFlow
      id="flow-supermemory"
      from="ChatGPT"
      to="Cursor"
      fact="Allergic to peanuts"
      others={['Claude', 'Codex']}
      caption="Something you tell ChatGPT is saved once to the encrypted memory on your computer, and Cursor and your other apps can recall it later."
    />
  </section>

  <section id="table">
    <h2>Side by side</h2>
    <table class="learn-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Supermemory</th><th scope="col">Zaaheen</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Made for</th><td>Mostly developers and teams building with AI memory</td><td>People who use several AI apps</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Where memories are kept</th><td>Hosted service, or a local folder with the local version</td><td>Your computer, encrypted</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Setup</th><td>Hosted: add a server address and sign in. Local: a terminal command</td><td>An installer, then connect each app</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Price</th><td>Free with $5 of credits a month; Pro $19 a month</td><td>{TRIAL.days} days free, then ${PRICES.monthly} a month or ${PRICES.yearly} a year</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Open source</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="learn-note">As of {CHECKED}, from Supermemory's own pages, and may change. Check the
       links above for the latest prices.</p>
    <p>What matters most in any memory is whether the right answer comes back, and whether the app
       admits it when the memory doesn't hold one. We asked Cursor five questions about facts another
       app had saved to a shared Zaaheen memory.</p>

    <BarChart
      id="chart-supermemory-cursor"
      title="5 questions to Cursor about facts saved by another app"
      measure="Questions"
      max={5}
      bars={[
        { label: 'Recalled the fact', value: 3, note: 'of 5' },
        { label: "Said it didn't know", value: 1, note: 'of 5' },
        { label: 'Avoided the trick', value: 1, note: 'of 5' },
      ]}
      source={{ text: "Zaaheen's own test, 5 June 2026, a development version of Zaaheen on Windows. Didn't know, correctly: a blood type the memory didn't hold. The trick: asked about the person's cat, it said they have a dog." }}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="better">
    <h2>Where Supermemory is the better choice</h2>
    <p>If you build AI products, Supermemory offers an API, connectors and team plans that Zaaheen
       doesn't. If you're comfortable in a terminal, its local version is free and open source. And
       its free hosted plan lets you try a shared memory without paying anything.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>Where Zaaheen falls short</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It runs on Windows and on Macs with Apple chips only, with no Linux or phone version.</li>
      <li>ChatGPT connects through the ChatGPT app for your computer, not in a web browser.</li>
      <li>Your memory lives on one computer; there is no sync between computers yet.</li>
      <li>It isn't open source, and after the {TRIAL.days}-day trial there is no free plan.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="learn-note">Supermemory is a name of its owner. We have no link with Supermemory.
       Facts checked on {CHECKED}; if anything here is wrong or out of date, tell us at
       <a href={`mailto:${COMPANY.email}`}>{COMPANY.email}</a> and we'll correct it.</p>
  </section>
</Learn>
